xnu 0427: skip the shared PCIe NoC SN7 vote in the WiFi sequence

Measured 2026-11-25: after the LDOs are left to the loader the worker reaches
xo.lvl and then wedges in the SN7 vote; the log never appears and the worker
never reaches "supplies complete". Earlier boots had voted SN7, so the direct
RSC access is contended or owned elsewhere, and the read does not return.

SN7 is the PCIe NoC bandwidth vote, not a power rail. The link trained with the
bootloader's NoC configuration, so keep that and skip the vote until there is
an owner-safe path through the AOP. The function stays for that work.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c b/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c
index 1a032c9..2fb984e 100644
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c
@@ -575,7 +575,7 @@ wifi_vote_xo(void)
 	return 0;
 }
 
-static int
+static int __unused
 wifi_vote_sn7(void)
 {
 	uint32_t addr = mondrian_cmd_db_addr("SN7");
@@ -626,9 +626,17 @@ wifi_supplies(void)
 	/* qcom,vdd-wlan-ipa-config has enable=0 on this phone. */
 	wifi_log("ipa rail smpe3 has DT enable=0; not voted");
 	if (wifi_vote_arc("mx.lvl") != 0 || wifi_vote_arc("cx.lvl") != 0 ||
-	    wifi_vote_xo() != 0 || wifi_vote_sn7() != 0) {
+	    wifi_vote_xo() != 0) {
 		return -1;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * SN7 is the PCIe NoC bandwidth vote. Measured 2026-11-25: the direct RSC
+	 * write wedges after xo.lvl and never returns, while earlier boots had
+	 * voted it. It is a bandwidth setting, not a power rail; the bootloader's
+	 * NoC configuration is what the link trained with. Skip it until the
+	 * owner-safe AOP path exists.
+	 */
+	wifi_log("noc SN7 vote skipped; loader NoC configuration retained");
 	delay(20000);
 	wifi_log("supplies complete; enabling WLAN");
 	return 0;
